   "source": [
    "## \uD83D\uDCE5 Phase 1: Live Multi-Source Data Ingestion (Extract — Bronze Layer)\n",
    "\n",
    "We are ingesting three foundational public policy data sources covering all U.S. counties:\n",
    "1. **U.S. Census Bureau ACS 5-Year API (`api.census.gov`)**: Live HTTP ingestion of county-level Median Gross Rent (`B25064_001E`), Poverty Universe & Counts (`B17001`), Adult Civilian & Veteran Populations (`B21001`), Housing Vacancy Counts (`B25002`), and Renter Tenure (`B25003`).\n",
    "2. **HUD Open Data ArcGIS REST API (`services.arcgis.com`) & HUD-VASH Administrative Allocation Records**: Live PHA telemetry paired with county-level HUD-VASH voucher issuance and leasing counts.\n",
    "3. **PRRAC Source of Income (SOI) Statutory Registry (`prrac.org` Appendix B)**: Empirical legal lookup table codifying state and county SOI anti-discrimination protections.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **\uD83C\uDF93 Teaching Moment — Production S3 Lakehouse vs. Live API Ingestion:**  \n",
    "> In an enterprise agency environment, scheduled jobs land raw extracts in an S3 Bronze bucket (`s3://mccourt-lab-data/...`). Notice that if you run `spark.read.csv(\"s3://non-existent-bucket/file.csv\")`, Spark prints `✅` without throwing an error! Why? Because **`spark.read` is a lazy transformation**. Spark does not touch storage until an **Action** (like `.saveAsTable()` in Phase 3) forces physical execution. Below, we fetch live federal data via HTTPS and land our raw **Bronze Delta Tables** so the entire pipeline is self-contained and reproducible."
   ]
  },
