   "source": [
    "## \uD83D\uDD2C Phase 5: Establishing Causality (Econometric OLS vs. Distributed PySpark MLlib)\n",
    "\n",
    "### \uD83C\uDF93 Teaching Moment — Choosing the Right Analytical Method for the Audience\n",
    "1. **Econometric Inference (`statsmodels` OLS with HC3 Robust Standard Errors):**  \n",
    "   * **Best for:** Academic seminars (McCourt School, NBER, APPAM) and Legislative testimony.  \n",
    "   * **Why:** Economists and policymakers care about **$\\hat{\\beta}_1$ (parameter identification, $p$-values, and confidence intervals)**—specifically, isolating the partial derivative of `utilization_rate` with respect to `has_soi_law` while holding confounding market characteristics (`median_rent`, `poverty_rate`, `vacancy_rate`, `veteran_pct`) constant:\n",
    "   $$\\text{UtilizationRate}_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 \\text{HasSOILaw}_i + \\beta_2 \\text{MedianRent}_i + \\beta_3 \\text{PovertyRate}_i + \\beta_4 \\text{VacancyRate}_i + \\beta_5 \\text{VeteranPct}_i + \\varepsilon_i$$\n",
    "   * **Why demonstrates Omitted Variable Bias (OVB):** Below, we run **Model 1 (Naive Bivariate OLS)** and **Model 2 (Controlled Multivariate OLS)**. Watch how the coefficient on `has_soi_law` jumps upward once we control for `median_rent`! Why? Because $\\text{Cov}(\\text{HasSOILaw}, \\text{MedianRent}) > 0$ and $\\beta_{\\text{MedianRent}} < 0$, causing negative Omitted Variable Bias in the naive model.\n",
    "2. **Distributed Machine Learning (`pyspark.ml` Pipeline & Random Forest / GBT):**  \n",
    "   * **Best for:** Federal agency operations (HUD / VA / OIG predictive risk targeting).  \n",
    "   * **Why:** When an agency needs to predict which PHAs will fail to lease vouchers next quarter across millions of household records, `pyspark.ml` executes distributed feature engineering and non-linear ensemble modeling across the cluster without pulling data to the single driver node."
   ]
  },
